(Phase 2 shared-library test corpus. Three exports cover the three things a
real library needs: pure arithmetic, printing, and buffer use. The buffer
function is the one that exercises the Phase 1 resource.asm PIC work —
buffer registration touches buf_table, the table that could not be relocated
before the lea [rel] rewrite. Built with `vox --shared`; called from
tests/runtime/shared_lib_driver.asm.

The arithmetic export is named with a space on purpose: "add two numbers"
mangles to the label `add_two_numbers`, so the export-set assertion in
test.sh exercises mangle_symbol instead of passing on an identity. See
plan 210 P2.

Phase 3 (plan 230, stage A1) adds the `Library` declaration: the export
labels become `<lib>_<ver>_<func>`, so "add two numbers" is now
`mathkit_1_0_add_two_numbers`. The driver's externs and test.sh's exact
export set move with it. Two libraries in one .so both defining `greet`
is what the mangling exists to allow; that case is exercised by a cargo
unit test, since the CLI cannot link two inputs until A2.

Stage A3 writes a `.lib` interface file beside the `.so` carrying each export's
signature, including its return type. The return type lives only in the `.lib`
— Vox source declares it in the body via a typed `Return a number, x.` — so the
returns here are typed rather than bare. That is byte-identical for the `.so`
(codegen only special-cases a `value` return), but the `.lib` would omit the
return type without it. `greet` has no Return and stays void, exercising the
parameterless-void entry `To "greet".` in the ToC.)

Library mathkit version "1.0".

To 'add two numbers' with a number called n.
  Return a number, n add 2.

To greet.
  Print "hello from libmath".

To makebuf.
  Create a buffer called b.
  Append "hello" to b.
  Return a number, b's size.